A robot drives a closed circuit. It measures its own motion between ticks by odometry, and the
landmarks it passes by a range sensor that returns their positions in its frame. The example
`pose_graph`, in the umbrella crate's `examples/`, builds that model once, draws a hundred datasets
from it, each with its own truth, and estimates all of them together: the mode of every dataset,
and a Laplace marginal for every pose and landmark. Then it asks whether those marginals are
honest, by counting how often the truth falls inside them. It runs in CI, and fails when a check
fails:

```sh
cargo run --release -p skoro --example pose_graph
```

<ExampleFigure
	name="pose-graph"
	alt="A closed circuit of 73 poses and 24 landmarks. Dead reckoning on the odometry drifts by up to 1.49 m; the estimate follows the dashed truth to within 34 cm, and a zoom at true scale shows each estimated pose with its 90% ellipse around the truth."
	caption="One of the hundred datasets: the truth, the start dead reckoning gives, and the estimate with its 90% ellipses."
/>

## The model, tick by tick

Each tick adds a pose, scored by a prior at the first tick and by the commanded motion from the
previous pose after it; the odometry, the relative motion between the two poses with an error; each
landmark seen for the first time, with a broad prior about its nominal position; and each
sighting, the landmark's position in the pose's frame with an error. Every term is a
[normal](../../concepts/the-normal/), on $SE(3)$ for the poses and the odometry and on $\R^3$ for the
rest:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro/examples/common/model.rs:24..78}
```

The same function serves [the sliding window](../sliding-window/), which calls it once per tick
in a build of its own. Which landmark each pose sights is fixed from the nominal trajectory, before
anything is drawn: the structure of a model is not random.

## A hundred datasets

The whole circuit is built in one build. Generating from it with nothing observed draws the truth
and its measurements together, a hundred rows of them, each its own dataset with the one structure.
The query observes the measurements, row by row, with `observe_value`, and each row starts where
dead reckoning on its own odometry puts it:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro/examples/common/batch.rs:27..61}
```

Since the truth is drawn from the model, the model is right by construction: what the example tests
is the estimation, not the modelling.

## The mode of every row

One linearizer and one schedule serve all hundred rows. `LevenbergMarquardt` finds each row's mode,
damping and stopping each row on its own, and `decrement` checks that every row stopped at a
stationary point of its density:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro/examples/pose_graph.rs:60..74}
```

Every row converges, in eight or nine iterations. A row's last step often promises less than the
rounding of its density, a few thousand nats here, and the method stops on that promise rather than
waiting for a gain it cannot measure.

## Are the uncertainties honest?

At the mode, the [Laplace approximation](../../concepts/linearization/#steps-marginals-and-the-laplace-approximation)
gives every pose and landmark a normal marginal in its own chart. The truth's tangent at the
estimate, from `Layout::local`, whitened by the marginal, is the normalized error; if the marginals
are calibrated it follows the chi-squared law with as many degrees of freedom as the quantity has
dimensions, and the 90% ellipsoid holds the truth nine times in ten, over datasets drawn from the
model. [The mathematics](../../archive/examples/math.svx#calibration) says why, and how large a
difference the approximation itself should make.

<ExampleFigure
	name="calibration"
	alt="Two histograms, of 7,300 pose errors and 2,400 landmark errors, lying on the chi-squared densities with 6 and 3 degrees of freedom; 92.0% of poses and 92.3% of landmarks fall inside their 90% ellipsoids, within the tolerances of 5.0 and 5.4 points."
	caption="The normalized errors of every pose and landmark of every dataset, against the chi-squared density each follows when its marginal is calibrated."
/>

The example checks it, with a tolerance fixed before it runs, not tuned to its result:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro/examples/pose_graph.rs:215..226}
```

## Drawing it

With `SKORO_FIGURES` naming a directory, the example also writes the data of these figures, and
`bin/figures` draws them:

```sh
SKORO_FIGURES=/tmp/figures cargo run --release -p skoro --example pose_graph
bin/figures /tmp/figures
```
